    "chapter_name": "Alternating Current",
    "year": "2022",
    "shift": "shift_1"
  },
  "question": {
    "text": "<p>For a series <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>L</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>R</mi></math>&nbsp;circuit, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi></math> vs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#969;</mi></math>&nbsp;curve is shown&nbsp;</p><p><img src=\"assets\\qt0071_cfc22ee9a450.png\" style=\"width: 210px; height: 178px;\" /></p><p>(a)&nbsp;To the left of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#969;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></math>, the circuit is mainly capacitive.<br />(b)&nbsp;To the left of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#969;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></math>, the circuit is mainly inductive.<br />(c)&nbsp;At&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#969;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></math>, impedance of the circuit is equal to the resistance of the circuit.<br />(d)&nbsp;At&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#969;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></math>, impedance of the circuit is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn></math>.</p><p>Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.</p>"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "(a) and (d) only",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "(b) and (d) only",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "(a) and (c) only",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "(b) and (c) only",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "C"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The impedance of an AC circuit is given by,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup></msqrt></math>.</p><p>For current to be maximum&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Z</mi></math>&nbsp;should be minimum. The value of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Z</mi></math>&nbsp;will be minimum when&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>. Therefore, the value of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi></math>. Hence, statement C is correct and statement D is wrong.</p><p>To the left of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#969;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></math>&nbsp;i.e. when&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#969;</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><msub><mi>&#969;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></math>,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></math>. Therefore, the circuit is mainly capacitive. Hence, statement A is correct and statement B is wrong.</p>"
  }
}